Evan had written a single word and covered it with his hand.

Miriam set her pencil down. The assistant stared at her blank paper.

Abby said, “I heard it through monitoring after the latch click, but the waveform places it during the earlier rattle. Headphone latency or my attention may explain the mismatch.”

Diane collected the slips face down.

Evan kept his. “We should compare.”

“After the position map and hardware inspection.”

“It sounds like a name.”

“Your slip preserves that hearing.”

“Whose name?”

Diane held out her hand.

He looked toward the sealed room, then gave her the paper.

From the corridor, the fire door settled once against its frame.

The sound carried no rattle this time.

Diane placed Evan’s folded slip beneath the others and closed the evidence envelope without reading any of them.

## Scene 6 — The Personal Reading

Diane opened the evidence envelope after Abby finished the position map.

They remained in the equipment alcove. The meter and flashlight stayed sealed inside the facilities case. Abby’s camera rested on the towel with its original card locked in a plastic holder beside it. On the workbench, she had drawn the main basement room in black pen: north panels, west door, fire door, floor drain, blue threshold line. Initials marked each person’s location before the first impact and after Diane ordered them back.

Evan stood at the far end of the bench. “We heard the clip. The map will not change the word.”

“We heard a sound,” Diane said.

She removed the folded slips and placed them in a row without opening them. Abby aimed the fresh camera card at Diane’s hands.

“Each person reads their own entry,” Diane said. “No discussion until all five are recorded.”

She unfolded hers first.

“Low vowel or throat sound. Source unlocated.”

Abby read: “Single voiced sound. Possible ‘uh’ or ‘down.’”

Miriam opened her slip. “Down.”

Her assistant’s paper remained blank.

Evan flattened his slip against the steel. One word occupied the center in block capitals.

DIANE.

He turned it toward the camera. “That is what it said.”

“That is what you heard,” Abby said.

Miriam looked at the five slips. “Three different records and one blank.”

“Three people heard a voice.”

“Two people recorded a word,” Diane said. “The words differ.”

Evan tapped his slip. “Mine fits the entire sequence.”

Diane collected the papers again. “Your word also matches the name repeated through the basement before the sound occurred.”

“You keep treating context as contamination.”

“It becomes contamination when you present recognition as independent.”

She sealed the slips in a second envelope and wrote the time across the flap. Evan watched the pen move.

Miriam said, “Diane’s name reached me upstairs. My assistant supplied it in the main room. I confirmed it afterward. The recording should preserve that order.”

Evan looked at her. “You still arrived at Deena.”

“After hearing Diane twice.”

“The door moved when you identified who it wanted.”

“The door moved after a long sequence that included the name, the crash story, the reflection test, a fan change, and a sound from the closer.”

His jaw shifted once. “You sound like her now.”

Miriam’s expression stayed level. “Accuracy is not allegiance.”

The assistant folded the blanket tighter over her arm.

Diane took the facilities flashlight from her belt and led them back to the main room. The tamper tape across the fire-door latch remained unbroken. Abby filmed it before Diane cut the strip. The room beyond carried the same sour trace from the drain. The three panel lights remained steady.

Diane opened the fire door while everyone stood in the corridor. She pushed it against the wall stop and watched the closer arm compress. Abby filmed the upper hardware. Evan crouched near the floor without crossing the threshold.

“Bottom clearance narrows near the stop,” he said.

Diane aimed her light across the concrete. A pale crescent showed where the door’s lower edge had scraped through floor dust.

“Floor contact confirmed,” Abby said.

Diane changed the supply fan from high to low at the wall control. Air pressure against the slab weakened. The door stayed against the stop. She returned the fan to high. The closer arm vibrated, and the slab moved a fraction of an inch before settling back.

Evan pointed. “There.”

“A fraction-of-an-inch movement under a fan change,” Diane said. “The earlier movement crossed half the opening.”

“So this fails to explain it.”

“It establishes one force acting on the door.”

She inspected the closer shoe, hinge screws, latch plate, wall stop, and sweep. The lower hinge carried old rust around one screw head. The closer arm showed fresh scoring where its two sections had flexed during the rattle. Nothing identified who or what had released the door from the stop.

Abby photographed each point.

Evan remained beside the threshold. “The hardware check leaves the main event unexplained.”

“It leaves the main event unresolved.”

“That is the same thing with less courage.”

Diane turned off the flashlight. “Everyone heard my name before the event. Your camera missed the hinge side. Abby’s frame missed the lower edge. The assistant stood nearest the door. The fan changed speed. The closer creaked. We have several forces and incomplete coverage.”

“And a voice that said your name.”

“One listener wrote my name.”

The assistant looked from Evan to the fire door. “Miriam still felt something turn toward Diane.”

Diane faced Miriam. “State exactly what remains.”

Miriam entered the room alone and stopped near the drain. Abby filmed from the corridor. Evan stayed behind the blue line after Diane pointed to it.

Miriam closed her eyes. Her right hand rested against her sternum. The supply fan pushed warm air across the open door and lifted the loose end of her scarf.

“I feel the same pressure near my throat,” she said. “Less anger. More attention.”

“Directed where?” Evan asked.

Miriam kept her eyes closed. “Toward Diane.”

Diane pressed her recorder marker. “What observation produces that conclusion?”

“When she speaks, the pressure shifts forward. When Evan speaks, it spreads through the room. When the assistant speaks, it weakens.”

“Could your attention account for that pattern?”

“Yes.”

“Could the conversation upstairs account for it?”

“Yes.”

“Could a presence account for it within your framework?”

“Yes.”

Evan raised his camera. “So ask what it knows about her.”

Miriam opened her eyes. “Only with Diane’s permission.”

Diane checked the camera positions, the open corridor, and the assistant’s hands gripping the blanket. “You may describe impressions. No questions to an assigned speaker. No names.”

Miriam nodded and closed her eyes again.

For several seconds, Diane heard only machinery: the fan’s steady push, water moving through the vertical pipe, a light electrical buzz from the north panels. Each sound held its place. None explained why every face had turned toward her.

Miriam said, “Something sweet. Cooked fruit. Dough or pastry.”

Evan glanced toward Diane.

“Keep your eyes on Miriam,” Diane said.

Miriam continued. “White blossoms, or a white pattern shaped like blossoms. A paper box. The smell belongs to a car before it belongs to a room.”

Evan’s mouth opened.

Diane raised one finger.

Miriam pressed her palm against the center of her chest. “Apple, perhaps. Cinnamon. Someone carrying the box while thinking about an older woman.”

Evan said, “Apple blossoms.”

Abby marked the time.

“The pastry,” Evan continued. “There was a box from Larkin’s on Diane’s passenger seat when we unloaded. Apple blossoms are their weekend special.”

Diane looked at him. “You saw the box.”

“I saw a bakery box.”

“The product name is printed across the side.”

His camera dipped a fraction.

The assistant asked, “Who was the older woman?”

Diane answered before anyone else could. “My mother. I took the box to her apartment before work. Two pastries stayed in my car.”

Evan turned toward Miriam. “You had no access to the car.”

“I walked past it in the lot,” Miriam said. “I cannot recall whether I saw the box.”

“You described the older woman.”

“Many people buy pastries for parents and grandparents.”

The assistant’s grip on the blanket loosened, then tightened again.

Miriam continued. “Dry stalks. Yellow dust. Rough cord against the palm.”

Evan said, “Hay.”

The assistant added, “A barn.”

Abby marked both contributions.

Miriam’s hand moved from her sternum to her left wrist. “A scrape here. Gloves removed too early. Animal heat, then cold air.”

Diane looked down at her coat cuff. A thin pale stem protruded from the seam where the fabric folded over her glove. She pulled it free and held it beneath Abby’s lens.

“Visible plant material on my clothing.”

Evan stepped closer. “From where?”

“My sister boards two horses outside Canaan Township. Her wrist is in a cast. I moved bedding and six square bales on Sunday.”

The assistant whispered, “That is exact.”

“The exact details came from me,” Diane said. “Miriam supplied dry stalks, cord, a scrape, and animals. You supplied hay and barn.”

Miriam opened her eyes. “Diane is correct.”

Evan lowered his camera enough to look over it. “She still perceived two private events from the same week.”

“One event was visible in my car. Evidence from the other is stuck to my sleeve.”

“Neither explains why those images came together.”

“They came together after you asked what something knew about me.”

Miriam faced Diane. “I also see metal in your hand. A smaller ring than the school keys. A button under your thumb. Cold around a car door.”

Evan glanced at the keys beneath Diane’s coat.

Diane drew out both rings. The school keys hung from the retractable line. Her car fob and two house keys sat on a separate steel loop.

“Visible since the door inspection,” she said.

Miriam nodded. “Yes.”

Evan pointed toward the car fob. “The crash.”

Diane’s fingers closed around both rings.

“The student from the wreck followed the keys,” he said. “That is why it shifted from the building to Diane.”

Miriam turned toward him. “You have joined separate impressions into one account.”

“The crash, her car, her name, the door.”

“You supplied the crash. The car image may come from the visible fob or the pastry box. I feel attention around Diane. I lack a verified identity for its source.”

The assistant stepped away from the fire door. “Could something have followed her home already?”

Diane returned the keys beneath her coat. “No evidence supports that.”

“But Miriam feels it around you.”

“Miriam reports a sensation in this room.”

Evan said, “You also spend nights here alone. That student wanted someone with keys who stays after everyone leaves.”

“The group assembled that description while looking at me.”

Miriam’s voice remained quiet. “When I focus on Diane, the impression feels more familiar and less fearful.”

“Because I am standing in front of you.”

“Possibly.”

“Because Evan told you my role.”

“Possibly.”

“Because something follows me.”

“Also possible within my practice.”

The assistant pressed the blanket against her ribs again. “Is it attached?”

Diane answered at once. “No one has established an attachment.”

Evan looked at Miriam.

Miriam shook her head. “I would not use that term from what I feel.”

“Following, then,” he said.

“I feel attention moving with Diane’s position inside this room.”

The fire door remained against the stop. The panel lights held steady. The sour smell near the drain weakened as water moved through the trap line.

Diane stepped into the corridor.

The assistant followed her with her eyes. Evan kept his camera on Miriam. No one moved toward the stairs.

Diane reached the end of the blue tape, turned, and looked at the group. Evan stood between the main room and the route out, though enough space remained beside him. The assistant’s face had lost color. Miriam watched Diane rather than the room.

